> setting). I am not a particularly fast typist so I don't notice a speed 
> difference; I feel like I make less typos in dvorak but never tested.
>
> At least do a little research about the differences before dismissing 
> learning Dvorak. Many places say Dvorak's faster (and back it up pretty 
> solidly), and some say there's no difference. But I've never seen a 
> source that claims Dvorak is slower, and I've looked. I've also never 
> heard of anyone saying they learned Dvorak and then switched back to 
> qwerty permanently.

I'm not claiming Dvorak is slower, or even that it has no benefits,
although I remain skeptical of these claims.  On the other hand, I *have*
done some research, e.g.

http://www.utdallas.edu/~liebowit/keys1.html

which casts much doubt on claims that have been made for Dvorak.

Perhaps sometime I'll try it out myself, but 
- I'm lazy
- I can already type at 70-80 wpm in qwerty with no discomfort, and my
  hands don't get tired as long as I hold them properly, so I really have
  no motivation to change beyond pure curiosity.
- I have a nagging fear that if I try another layout I'll become more apt
  to make typos in either layout.

